Step 1 — Create (or check) your profile repository

Your GitHub profile README only works if your repository name matches your GitHub username exactly.

👉 Example:

If your GitHub username is sandipsaw,
then your repo name must be sandipsaw.

✅ To create it:
Go to https://github.com/new
In Repository name, type your GitHub username (e.g., sandipsaw)
Make sure it’s Public
Tick ✅ “Add a README file”
Click Create repository

Step 2 — Create the GitHub Actions workflow
Inside your repo, click Add file → Create new file
Name the file exactly like this:

.github/workflows/snake.yml
(That means: a folder .github, inside it a folder workflows, and inside that, a file snake.yml)

Paste this code inside:
------------------------------------------------
name: Generate snake animation

on:
  schedule: # execute every 12 hours
    - cron: "* */12 * * *"
  workflow_dispatch:
  push:
    branches:
      - main

jobs:
  generate:
    permissions:
      contents: write
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    timeout-minutes: 5

    steps:
      - name: generate snake.svg
        uses: Platane/snk/svg-only@v3
        with:
          github_user_name: ${{ github.repository_owner }}
          outputs: dist/snake.svg?palette=github-dark

      - name: push snake.svg to the output branch
        uses: crazy-max/ghaction-github-pages@v3.1.0
        with:
          target_branch: output
          build_dir: dist
        env:
          GITHUB_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
----------------------------------------------------------
Scroll down and click Commit changes.

Step 3 — Run the workflow manually
Go to the Actions tab in your repository.
Click on “Generate snake animation” (you’ll see it listed on the left).
On the right side, click Run workflow → Run workflow.
Wait 1–4 minutes until it finishes.
You’ll know it’s successful when it shows ✅ Success (green check mark).

Step 4 — Check for the generated snake
Go to the Code tab of your repository.
Click the branch dropdown (where it says main) → select output.
You should see:

dist/snake.svg
That’s your animated contribution graph!

Step 5 — Copy the raw link to the snake
Open the file dist/snake.svg
Click Raw
Copy the link from your browser’s address bar —
it will look like this:

https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sandipsaw/sandipsaw/output/snake.svg

Step 6 — Add it to your README
Switch back to your main branch
Edit your README.md
Paste this code where you want the snake to appear:

![GitHub Contribution Snake](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sandipsaw/sandipsaw/output/snake.svg)

Step 7 — See it live!
Go to your GitHub profile page:
👉 https://github.com/sandipsaw
You’ll now see the snake moving across your contribution graph 🐍💚

(Optional) Step 8 — Make it look perfect in both dark & light themes
Replace the Markdown in your README with this HTML code:

------------------------------------------------------------------------
<picture>
  <source srcset="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sandipsaw/sandipsaw/output/snake.svg?palette=github-dark" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
  <img alt="GitHub Contribution Snake" src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/sandipsaw/sandipsaw/output/snake.svg">
</picture>
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Now, users in dark mode will see the dark snake 🐍,
and those in light mode will see the light one 🐍✨
